WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.360
High Medieval guilds in Western Europe didn’t&nbsp;
come into existence out of nothing. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:17.120
an evolution of a fraternity or a collegium&nbsp;
system dating back to perhaps as far as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.720
Roman Empire but certainly at least to the&nbsp;
early Middle Ages. If you want to know more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.520
about early medieval ‘gilds’, I have made a&nbsp;
video about that which you can watch here.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.920
Despite their connections to the early medieval&nbsp;
guilds, the guilds of the high Middle Ages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:36.440
from the 11th century onwards, were quite&nbsp;
different compared to their earlier counterparts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:41.920
Early medieval guilds were sparse and resembled&nbsp;
more religious fraternities and drinking clubs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.040
whereas high medieval guilds became more&nbsp;
centred around a single occupation and were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.400
far more numerous. The proliferation of high&nbsp;
medieval guilds compared to earlier ones can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.960
be attributed to the increasing&nbsp;
urbanisation of Western Europe.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.560
After the fall of Rome in the 5th century&nbsp;
and the subsequent migration period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:07.120
urban centres of Western Europe substantially&nbsp;
shrank in size as food production mostly switched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:12.720
from the large-scale Roman villa farming system&nbsp;
to much smaller subsistence farming that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.680
not support large growing urban centres. The&nbsp;
6th century also saw the Plague of Justinian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.560
and a volcanic winter all adding to the fact&nbsp;
why a lot of urban centres during this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:29.960
period saw a decrees in population. However, this&nbsp;
started to change by the time of the 10th and 11th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:35.600
centuries. The expansion of farmland due to new&nbsp;
farm technologies, the ‘medieval warm’ period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.200
and the firm establishment of the feudal farming&nbsp;
system created a surplus of food that could once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:47.320
again support large and growing urban centres. On&nbsp;
top of the growing population in urban centres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.400
the opening of new trade routes, which&nbsp;
was thanks in part to the Vikings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:56.080
the emergence of the Islamic caliphate, and the&nbsp;
medieval slave trade, facilitated an increase in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:02.360
the production of goods in European urban areas&nbsp;
which now had access to more and bigger markets.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:08.120
The increasing food supply, urban population,&nbsp;
and trade meant that 11th-century Western Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:13.680
was a fertile ground for the creation of guilds&nbsp;
which, rather than being social or religious in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.960
character, like the early medieval guilds&nbsp;
were, centred more around a single craft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:24.720
job, or occupation. In other words, because&nbsp;
towns and cities started to have far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.840
people that shared the same occupation, it&nbsp;
was only natural that these people used the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:36.400
known guild system to establish institutions for&nbsp;
the sake of furthering their jobs and careers.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.880
The structure and operation of these guilds&nbsp;
differed depending on their geographical and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:47.040
temporal locations, but at their most&nbsp;
basic, a guild was ‘an association of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:52.360
people who shared certain interests and&nbsp;
wished to pursue common purposes.’ This,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:58.240
quote, ‘association of people’ could have been&nbsp;
as simple as a list of artisans from a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.960
town that agreed on some production standards&nbsp;
or as complicated as the Hanseatic League,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.000
which was technically a merchant guild&nbsp;
composed of solely other merchant guilds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.240
all of which could have different contracts with&nbsp;
the Hanseatic League while having different guild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:20.240
structures within themselves. In other words,&nbsp;
just like today’s companies can be both a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:26.880
conglomerate like Alphabet or a small 10 men&nbsp;
operation, so could medieval guilds be a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:33.400
multi-kingdom conglomerate like the Hanseatic&nbsp;
League or a small-town association of 10 bakers.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:37.960
The formation of the most common type of&nbsp;
medieval guild was quite simple. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:43.240
in a town who shared a common occupation got&nbsp;
together and agreed to follow some rules and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:48.120
standards regarding their occupation. These&nbsp;
rules could have been as simple as saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.480
every member of a tailoring guild can only make&nbsp;
pillows and mattresses in this one specific way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:58.480
or they could have been as complicated as&nbsp;
controlling every aspect of the artisan’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.400
work. Some guilds had rules that dictated&nbsp;
when the members were allowed to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:08.360
what they were allowed to make, from whom they&nbsp;
were allowed to buy materials, to whom they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.560
allowed to sell their products, whom they were&nbsp;
allowed to hire, how they have to fire people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.880
what kind of insurance they can get, etc.&nbsp;
Basically, a medieval guild could have had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.440
rules pertaining to every aspect of your&nbsp;
occupation and sometimes even your life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:31.000
Some guilds had rules on when you were allowed to&nbsp;
marry or how many times a week you must pray, etc.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.840
Considering how much control a&nbsp;
guild could have over its members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:41.200
why would artisans want to create or join such&nbsp;
institutions rather than work independently? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.600
the reasons were many, but the most important&nbsp;
one was collective bargaining. If you were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:51.840
let’s say, a fisherman in 12th century Worms,&nbsp;
and you were getting priced out of the market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:57.280
by cheaper imported fish, as an individual, there&nbsp;
wouldn’t be much you could do about it . However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:03.440
as part of an ‘association’, a guild composed of&nbsp;
all the fishermen in Worms, suddenly you had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:09.320
collective bargaining power to lobby the ruler&nbsp;
of Worms to give the Worms’ Fishermen’s guild the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:15.600
exclusive rights to control the wholesale market&nbsp;
of fish in the city and not allow imported fish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.680
At the same time, due to this exclusive deal, any&nbsp;
fisherman in Worms not part of the Fishermen’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:27.200
Guild would either have to join the guild or&nbsp;
not be able to sell fish in Worms anymore.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:32.520
An example of a similar deal happened in&nbsp;
12th-century London, where King Henry I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:38.800
gave the London Weavers Guild an exclusive right&nbsp;
to practice their craft in London. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.200
only the members of the weavers guild&nbsp;
could practice weaving in London.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.680
Besides collective bargaining and market&nbsp;
control, Guilds could also provide other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.880
benefits to their members. Some guilds,&nbsp;
on top of acting as occupation guilds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:58.320
also provided a healthy sociable character with&nbsp;
frequent events often involving heavy drinking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:04.000
These events often happened at guild halls which&nbsp;
were paid for by the dues of the guild members.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:09.440
Guilds sometimes even built chapels for their&nbsp;
members to prey in or bought buildings where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.120
tools and materials could be stored. Plus, on top&nbsp;
of market control rights, guilds often persuaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.800
rulers to give their members tax cuts or lower&nbsp;
import and export dues. So these occupation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.960
guilds initially started as collective&nbsp;
bargaining associations and support groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:30.640
but they quickly evolved into institutions that&nbsp;
could control entire markets in towns and cities.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:34.960
Why did, however, rulers give these&nbsp;
exclusive rights to the guilds?&nbsp;

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:40.480
Well, First: if the guild is large enough to&nbsp;
have most of the, let’s say, weavers of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:46.880
city as members, them threatening to stop working&nbsp;
could be a big issue. Not having enough cloth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:51.320
especially during winter, could cause&nbsp;
substantial problems for the ruler.&nbsp;

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:57.320
Second: bribery. A lot of the charters given to&nbsp;
guilds by rulers that gave the guilds certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:02.560
rights also often stipulated some kind of payment&nbsp;
on the part of the guild. This could have been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:07.640
one-time payment or a continuing payment.&nbsp;
The weaving guild of London, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:14.240
paid Henry 16 pounds up front and another 1 pound&nbsp;
and 33 pence annually. Plus, there was certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.640
some back-door exchange of money or favours for&nbsp;
these exclusive deals happening as well that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.880
we just don’t know about.
Third: these guilds were pretty helpful to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:28.840
rulers for controlling the market. It was easier&nbsp;
for rulers to negotiate directly with guilds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:36.520
enforce price adjustments through them rather than&nbsp;
dealing with thousands of craftsmen individually.&nbsp;

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:42.640
Fourth: sometimes guilds, like the Hanseatic&nbsp;
League, got so powerful that rulers couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:47.960
really say no to their demands because if they&nbsp;
did, they would find themselves at war with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:53.920
mercenary army hired by the guild. On the other&nbsp;
hand, if a ruler had a very good standing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:59.000
a guild, the guild could sometimes help the&nbsp;
ruler in a war by hiring mercenaries for them.&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:10.600
These are the reasons why rulers often&nbsp;
gave very generous rights to many guilds.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:14.840
What was the structure of these&nbsp;
medieval guilds? I am glad you asked.&nbsp;

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:19.840
The most basic structure of a medieval&nbsp;
guild looked like this. At the very top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:25.120
you had Masters of the Guild. These were&nbsp;
people who were deemed to have ‘mastered’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:30.360
the occupation of the guild and were often the&nbsp;
only ones who could employ other members of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:36.560
guild. Masters either ran the affairs of the&nbsp;
guild altogether in a sort of direct democracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.200
or they voted in members from amongst&nbsp;
each other into a ruling council in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:46.400
sort of representative democracy. Under&nbsp;
the masters, there were the journeymen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:51.960
also called bachelors. Journeymen were members&nbsp;
of the guild who had skills in the relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:57.760
occupation and who could work for masters for a&nbsp;
wage but who still did not have the title of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:03.040
master and could not employ other members of the&nbsp;
guild. Lastly, there were apprentices who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:08.240
people being trained in the guild’s occupation&nbsp;
while working under one of the masters. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:13.800
guilds had slightly different structures compared&nbsp;
to this, but this was the most common type.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:19.360
From the 13th century onwards, guilds were so&nbsp;
well established across Western Europe that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:25.000
almost all towns and cities, the only way to&nbsp;
become a merchant, a craftsman, or a lawyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:30.000
was to sign up with a guild and go through&nbsp;
the guild’s training process. Each guild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.280
set its own rules and criteria for how this&nbsp;
training process looked, but the structure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:40.920
the process remained largely the same across&nbsp;
all the guilds. This process worked thusly.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:46.440
First, as an untrained urban dweller, you had to&nbsp;
find a master in the specific occupation guild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:51.520
you wanted to work in. Once you found one, you&nbsp;
then had to convince the master to take you on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:56.840
as his apprentice. This master could decide on his&nbsp;
own whether to accept you or not, but often guilds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:02.960
had rules set up about whom masters could or could&nbsp;
not take as apprentices. There were often rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:06.600
about feudal status (no peasants), marital status&nbsp;
(not married), age range (usually between 14-35),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:09.440
gender (females were mostly not allowed), religion&nbsp;
(only Christians), and by the time of the 16th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:13.840
century even ethnicity (usually no “outsiders”&nbsp;
allowed). If you, however, qualified under all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:19.200
these criteria and a master had agreed to take&nbsp;
you on, you were allowed to join the guild as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.640
an apprentice. Usually, there was a fee you&nbsp;
had to pay to join the guild, and there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:28.360
further payments throughout your training you&nbsp;
had to give to the master for teaching you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:33.840
You sometimes got paid for your apprenticeship&nbsp;
work, but this was often very little. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:39.240
apprentices usually had multiple jobs in&nbsp;
order to pay for all the apprenticeship fees.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:45.080
After becoming an apprentice, each guild set&nbsp;
its own requirements for how long one had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:50.440
be an apprentice before becoming a journeyman,&nbsp;
also known as a bachelor. This could be anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:56.240
from 2 years to as long as 10 years. Once&nbsp;
you had completed the required time set by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:00.880
the guild and learned the skills of the trade,&nbsp;
you became a journeyman. Meaning you could now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:05.760
work in the specific occupation of the guild&nbsp;
you just trained in under any master you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:11.040
for a set wage determined by the guild. You&nbsp;
can also travel to other places in Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:16.280
to work under various different masters and&nbsp;
learn new skills. Guilds across cities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:22.120
kingdoms often had contracts that recognised the&nbsp;
training of each other’s journeymen and masters.

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:27.000
Becoming a master is a bit more complicated.&nbsp;
Some guilds had hereditary masterships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:32.000
meaning you could not become a master unless your&nbsp;
father was a master, while others had requirements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:37.840
like you having to be married or having enough&nbsp;
money to buy a workshop, or simply having to wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:43.880
until some master died because there was a quota&nbsp;
on how many masters the guild could have. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:49.120
you also had to pay another fee to become a&nbsp;
master, and you also had to make a piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:54.800
work relevant to your guild’s occupation. This&nbsp;
piece of work was called a ‘masterpiece’ and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.600
was judged by the current masters of the guild&nbsp;
to see if you were skilled enough to become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:04.880
master. This was the most common way one would&nbsp;
work through a guild’s membership structure.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:10.200
Medieval guilds could have been an association of&nbsp;
people doing any kind of occupation. There were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:16.760
innkeeper guilds, merchant guilds, lawyer guilds,&nbsp;
gardener guilds, cobbler guilds, baker guilds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:21.920
etc. Sometimes these guilds could have been&nbsp;
extremely specific, like a guild of tailors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:27.960
that made only mattress and pillow covers or&nbsp;
a guild of blacksmiths who made only scissors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:33.360
also known as scissorsmiths. There were&nbsp;
even guilds of only teachers and educators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:39.080
better known today as universities. In&nbsp;
fact, the word university, or universitas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:43.560
was just a different name for a guild&nbsp;
which over the course of the middle ages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:47.400
became synonymous with only teaching&nbsp;
and education guilds (exceptions apply).

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:53.400
Medieval universities, like Oxford and Cambridge,&nbsp;
function pretty much exactly as any other medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:59.720
guild did. The guild, i.e. the university, was&nbsp;
composed of masters who set the rules and laws&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:05.400
for the guild and who had apprentices for&nbsp;
4 years, i.e. the students. The students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:10.800
just like in other guilds, payed some sort of&nbsp;
fee to the masters and the guild in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:16.640
be taught. Once a student graduated, he was a&nbsp;
journeyman also known as a bachelor. A bachelor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:21.880
within the guild could work for a master as&nbsp;
a teacher and a lecturer. A bachelor also got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:26.560
a diploma which stated that he attained&nbsp;
his bachelorship, and with that diploma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:30.880
just like in any other guild, he could go to&nbsp;
other universities around Europe, which had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:37.480
agreements with your original university, to teach&nbsp;
there under different masters. After 3 years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:42.920
teaching, the bachelor could apply to the head&nbsp;
of the guild, the chancellor of the university,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:48.880
to be appointed a master. If you passed your test&nbsp;
and the chancellor and other masters of the guild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:55.280
agreed to give you the title, you became a master&nbsp;
in the university. As a master in the guild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.280 --> 00:14:00.880
you could now take on new apprentices i.e.&nbsp;
students, and you could higher bachelors to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:07.000
you teach these new students you were taking on.
Therefore, it is the medieval guild system from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:12.280
which we get our modern ideas of a university&nbsp;
degree which is recognised by other universities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:17.720
and the names for those degrees, i.e. a&nbsp;
bachelor’s degree and a master’s degree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:23.440
The idea of a Doctorate as something more than&nbsp;
a master’s was a later development that did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:34.080
exist in the Middle Ages .
 
“Were guilds good?”

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:39.120
In modern scholarship, medieval guilds&nbsp;
are highly debated. This is because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:44.120
were complicated and possessed both&nbsp;
positive and negative attributes.

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:48.920
On the positive side, guilds established&nbsp;
production standards in order that no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:55.280
tradesman could scam his customers by delivering&nbsp;
a poorly made product. Some guilds were so intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.280 --> 00:15:01.240
in enforcing the production standards that they&nbsp;
would outright destroy the inferior products. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:06.760
example, brewers’ guilds were known to pour out&nbsp;
barrels of beer that didn’t meet their standards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:12.440
At a time when the state did not regulate the&nbsp;
standards of consumable products , guilds with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:17.680
production standards definitely saved some&nbsp;
people’s lives. It could also be argued that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:23.520
guilds, with their frequent lobbying of rulers,&nbsp;
forced medieval rulers to rely less on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:30.200
rural feudal farming population and become more&nbsp;
invested in economically productive urban centres.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:35.720
Lastly, the most praised positive aspect of&nbsp;
guilds was their facilitation of knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:41.800
communication. Some historians like David de la&nbsp;
Croix and others state that guilds’ systems of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.800 --> 00:15:47.960
apprenticeship and journeymanship facilitated&nbsp;
the communication of valuable information not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:53.400
just from one generation to the next but also&nbsp;
across borders. Through the apprenticeship system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:59.000
a new generation of labourers could be effectively&nbsp;
trained, and through the journeyman system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:03.560
where people travelled across Europe to work&nbsp;
under various masters, valuable industrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:09.440
information could be communicated across borders.&nbsp;
Proponents of this theory go as far as to argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:14.160
that this aspect of medieval guilds was the&nbsp;
reason why Europe emerged from the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:19.320
ages economically ahead when compared&nbsp;
to other areas of the world like China.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:25.240
For all the positives the guild system provided,&nbsp;
however, there were also many negatives.

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:30.480
As already stated, medieval guilds often&nbsp;
lobbied rulers to give them monopolistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:35.760
rights over their trade, which drastically&nbsp;
hindered competition and innovation. Guilds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:40.480
also often enforced production quotas-&nbsp;
artificially creating market scarcity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:46.400
and raising consumer prices. They also often had&nbsp;
limits on the number of masters and apprentices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:52.240
along with sometimes extremely strict entry&nbsp;
requirements, which often excluded 90% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:57.280
the population. Unsurprisingly in such&nbsp;
a system, nepotism was very frequent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:03.160
with most new guild members coming from families&nbsp;
that already had at least one member in the guild.

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:08.720
An interesting side note here, ethnic berries&nbsp;
for guild memberships weren’t very common in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:13.560
the middle ages but became increasingly&nbsp;
common from the 16th century onwards .

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:17.480
With that said, without a doubt,&nbsp;
the most excluded group from guild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:21.920
membership were women. Out of all the&nbsp;
guilds we have surviving records for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:28.680
only 0.5% of them accepted women , 60%&nbsp;
of which were in France. A 13th-century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:34.240
French guild master even complained that girls&nbsp;
should be entirely barred from guild memberships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:39.040
‘because girls were leaving their fathers and&nbsp;
mothers, and beginning to practice their craft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:44.560
and taking on apprentices, and doing nothing&nbsp;
other than leading debauched lives.’ I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:49.360
how dare girls have agency and social and&nbsp;
economic mobility, am I right! (sarcasm)

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:53.680
Ogilvie probably summarised it the&nbsp;
best by stating that guilds were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:58.640
‘reserved for a privileged minority of&nbsp;
the urban population … Guilds were not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:03.280
all-encompassing workers’ associations&nbsp;
analogous to twentieth-century labor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:09.000
unions but exclusive organisations for&nbsp;
relatively well-off, middle-class men.’

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:15.840
In his famous book The Wealth of Nations, Adam&nbsp;
Smith even stated that guilds were ‘a conspiracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:21.360
against the public’ and that the government&nbsp;
should not allow such institutions to exist.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:24.960
So what do you think? Were&nbsp;
medieval guilds a good thing?

